WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:04.770
Songwriting is
tension and release.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:07.290
You set up an
expectation, and then you

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:08.730
make people wait just a second--

00:00:10.898 --> 00:00:12.440
and then you fulfill
the expectation.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.482
[MUSIC PLAYING - ST. VINCENT,
 "FEAR THE FUTURE"]

00:00:14.982 --> 00:00:17.490
Your best tool in the
world is your ears.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.790
Da da da da dee dee
dee dee dee dee dee.

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:22.640
If you have ears and ideas,
you can make anything happen.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:27.340
As an artist, you will
never be satisfied.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.440
The trick is to
take something good

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:34.570
and bludgeon it till it's great,
or set it aside and refine,

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:36.600
refine, refine, refine, refine.

00:00:36.985 --> 00:00:39.070
In this MasterClass, I
want to share with you guys

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:43.510
some performance tips, some
songwriting tips, some studio

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:44.740
tips.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:46.510
We're going to take
my song "Savior"

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:50.410
and walk through every step
of the songwriting process.

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:53.950
You can hear how it's doing a
kind of rhythm, [SINGS RHYTHM]

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:55.420
to add texture.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:58.240
The first lyric really ought
to be compelling enough

00:00:58.240 --> 00:00:59.860
for people to go, wait, what?

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:01.990
(SINGING) You dress
me up, please.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:07.850
Got that?

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:10.310
Are Annie Clark and St.
Vincent the same thing?

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:12.110
Honestly, you'd have to ask her.

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:14.210
I came up with the
moniker St. Vincent

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:16.490
so that I could
have the flexibility

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:19.220
to be any number of things.

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:22.160
I recorded a lot of my first
album in my childhood bedroom,

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:24.036
sitting at the
computer like this.

00:01:24.036 --> 00:01:24.710
[TYPING]

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:26.450
I'm going to play the audio.

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:28.550
It's so embarrassing.

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:30.450
Oh god.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.210
Make shame your friend.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:35.420
Because there's no way to get
good at something without being

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:37.040
bad at it first.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.410
Just keep going, because
everybody feels weird

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:39.980
sometimes.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:41.780
Everybody feels like
they don't fit in,

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:45.110
but you can speak to that
in a way that's real.

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:48.230
You can move the needle in
a more empathetic direction

00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:50.600
for the world.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:52.270
I'm St. Vincent,

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:53.580
and I'm Annie Clark,

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.360
and this is my MasterClass.

